### What is a mutex?

A mutex enforces mutual exclusion: only one thread can hold the lock at a time, and only the owning thread can release it. In Java, both `synchronized` and `ReentrantLock` implement mutex semantics.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class MutexDemo {
    private static final ReentrantLock mutex = new ReentrantLock();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Thread-A owns the lock
        mutex.lock();     // holdCount = 1
        mutex.lock();     // REENTRANT: holdCount = 2 (no deadlock)
        mutex.unlock();   // holdCount = 1
        mutex.unlock();   // holdCount = 0, released

        // Thread-B trying to unlock Thread-A's lock:
        // mutex.unlock(); → IllegalMonitorStateException: not the owner!
    }
}
```

### What is a semaphore?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

public class SemaphoreDemo {
    private static final Semaphore sem = new Semaphore(3);  // 3 permits

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        sem.acquire();  // Thread-A takes a permit (3 → 2)
        sem.acquire();  // Thread-A takes another (2 → 1)

        // Thread-B releases a permit (1 → 2): ALLOWED! No ownership check!
        new Thread(() -> {
            sem.release();  // no exception! any thread can release
            System.out.println("Thread-B released a permit");
        }).start();
    }
}
```

### How are they compared?

| Feature | Mutex (Lock/synchronized) | Semaphore |
|---|---|---|
| Ownership | Yes (only owner unlocks) | No (any thread releases) |
| Reentrant | Yes | No (re-acquire blocks) |
| Concurrent holders | 1 (mutual exclusion) | N (counting permits) |
| Wrong-thread release | IllegalMonitorStateException | Silently succeeds |
| Purpose | Protect critical section | Limit access, signal between threads |
| Java class | ReentrantLock, synchronized | Semaphore |
| Banking use case | Balance update | Connection pool limit |

### When should you use each?

Use a mutex when you need to protect a critical section where exactly one thread should execute at a time, and the same thread that enters must be the one that exits. Use a semaphore when you need to limit the number of concurrent accessors to a bounded resource or signal between threads.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class UseCaseDemo {
    // Mutex: protect balance update (ownership matters)
    private static final ReentrantLock balanceLock = new ReentrantLock();
    private static BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    static void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balanceLock.lock();
        try {
            balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        } finally {
            balanceLock.unlock();  // same thread that locked
        }
    }

    // Semaphore: limit DB connections (counting, no ownership)
    private static final Semaphore dbPool = new Semaphore(10);

    static void query() throws InterruptedException {
        dbPool.acquire();
        try {
            // max 10 concurrent DB queries
        } finally {
            dbPool.release();
        }
    }
}
```

### Why should you never use binary semaphore as a mutex?

Because there is no ownership check, another thread can accidentally (or intentionally) release the semaphore, breaking mutual exclusion. Two threads would then be inside the critical section simultaneously.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does Java have a Mutex class? No. synchronized and ReentrantLock serve as mutexes. Binary Semaphore is NOT a true mutex because it lacks ownership.
- Can a semaphore have more releases than acquires? Yes. If you call release() without a prior acquire(), the permit count increases beyond its initial value, which is usually a bug.
